IW620: a remain-on-channel request no longer waits for ever on a firmware that stopped answering

Applies to nxp-imx/mwifiex lf-6.18.2_1.0.0 with ps5-linux-mwifiex's patches (build.sh adds it after theirs).

Issue #60 (ziernaht): "Timeout cmd id 802_11_REMAIN_ON_CHANNEL [0x10d]", "IOCTL: command timeout", then wpa_supplicant
blocked in woal_wait_ioctl_complete <- woal_set_remain_channel_ioctl <- woal_cfg80211_authenticate, and every process
that touched the network in D state until the console was restarted.

With host MLME the driver sends authentication frames itself and asks the firmware to stay on the channel first. That
request waited with MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT. For a command that times out, woal_ioctl_complete() prints "IOCTL: command
timeout" and leaves a MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT caller asleep, expecting a firmware reload to finish the request; the PS5 runs
with auto_fw_reload=0. cfg80211 holds the wiphy mutex across the call and NetworkManager holds RTNL while it waits for
that mutex, so all networking hung behind it.

A MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT_TIMEOUT caller is woken on completion whatever the status, and after 20 s at the latest. The request
stays allocated while it is pending (the existing "ret != MLAN_STATUS_PENDING" in this function) and its wait queue
lives in that allocation, so a late completion finds valid memory. The caller gets an error and the connection attempt
fails, where it used to freeze the console. Same approach as ps5-iw620-rtnl-bounded-wait.patch for the station queries.

moal_ioctl.o compiles against the PS5 kernel tree (7.3.3 + series) without warnings. Not run on a console.
---
diff --git a/mlinux/moal_ioctl.c b/mlinux/moal_ioctl.c
index 22be50b..effcc10 100644
--- a/mlinux/moal_ioctl.c
+++ b/mlinux/moal_ioctl.c
@@ -5394,6 +5394,16 @@ mlan_status woal_set_remain_channel_ioctl(moal_private *priv, t_u8 wait_option,
 	moal_memcpy_ext(priv->phandle, &radio_cfg->param.remain_chan, pchan,
 			sizeof(mlan_ds_remain_chan),
 			sizeof(mlan_ds_remain_chan));
+	/* cfg80211 gets here with the wiphy mutex held (authenticate sends its
+	 * frames through remain-on-channel), and NetworkManager can hold RTNL
+	 * while it waits for that mutex. When the firmware stops answering,
+	 * the command times out, and woal_ioctl_complete() does not wake a
+	 * MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT caller for a command timeout: wpa_supplicant stayed
+	 * here for good and every network call on the console blocked behind
+	 * it. A MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT_TIMEOUT caller is woken with the error.
+	 */
+	if (wait_option == MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT)
+		wait_option = MOAL_IOCTL_WAIT_TIMEOUT;
 	ret = woal_request_ioctl(priv, req, wait_option);
 	if (ret == MLAN_STATUS_SUCCESS) {
 		moal_memcpy_ext(priv->phandle, pchan,
